help_knot() {
  cat <<'EOF'
usage: valley knot <verb> [args]

  knot <verb> [args]
                   read and write the project's plan graph: knots, at
                   refs/the-valley/knots on origin. a knot is an outcome,
                   a bug, an idea or a question; `knot schema` prints the
                   types, their statuses, active and terminal, the fields
                   and the edges. a write is one commit on the ref, made
                   without a checkout and pushed with a lease on the tip
                   it was made on; no review is involved. a write that
                   loses the lease is made again on the new tip, up to 5
                   tries. every verb takes --project NAME, another
                   project on the same host, and --json. exit status 3
                   is a conflict. needs the valley-go helper, and
                   git config valley.principal for every write.
                     new <type> <title> [--parent ID] [--blocked-by ID]...
                         [--owner P] [--target PROJECT] [--body -]
                                  make a knot. its id is the type's prefix
                                  and 7 hex digits of the sha-256 of the
                                  title's slug.
                     show <id> [--at COMMIT] [--json]
                                  its fields, body and comments, each
                                  comment with its id, and the edges both
                                  ways. --at shows the knot at a commit of
                                  the graph; claim age uses the current
                                  clock, and other projects' knots use
                                  their current tips.
                     log <id> [--json]
                                  every commit of the graph that touched
                                  the knot or its comments, newest first:
                                  time, committer, operation, and the
                                  files it added, changed or deleted
                     list [--type T] [--status S] [--owner P] [--ready]
                                  every knot. --status takes a status,
                                  active or terminal. --ready lists the
                                  active, unclaimed knots whose blockers
                                  are all terminal.
                     set <id> <field>=<value>... [--if <field>=<value>]...
                         [--force]
                                  change status, title, source, owner,
                                  target or graduated_into. a field that
                                  another write changed meanwhile is a
                                  conflict unless --force. --if requires
                                  a field to hold a value first.
                     link <id> (--blocked-by ID | --parent ID | --change X)...
                     unlink <id> (--blocked-by ID | --parent ID | --change X)...
                                  add or drop an edge. a change is
                                  valley:<project>/<change id> or a github
                                  pull request url. outcomes and bugs link
                                  the changes that do the work, and a
                                  question links the change that records
                                  its answer in the source.
                     edit <id> --body -
                                  replace the body with stdin
                     comment <id> (-m TEXT | --body -) [--edit COMMENT-ID]
                                  add a comment. --edit replaces the body
                                  of the comment with that id; its author
                                  and file stay, and it records who edited
                                  it and when. only the comment's author
                                  or a holder of the approve grant may
                                  edit it. a comment that another edit
                                  changed meanwhile is a conflict.
                                  comments are never deleted; log and
                                  show --at read every earlier text.
                     claim <id> --run RUN
                                  claim an active outcome or bug for a
                                  run; an open outcome becomes
                                  in-progress. a knot claimed already is
                                  a conflict.
                     unclaim <id>
                                  release the claim. a claim that
                                  changes before the release lands is
                                  a conflict.
                     schema [--json]
                                  print the model
EOF
  help_runtime
}

help_plan() {
  cat <<'EOF'
usage: valley plan [--project NAME | --all] [--owner P] [--json] [--known-signers <file>] [--integrator-name <name>]

  plan [--project NAME | --all] [--owner P] [--json]
       [--known-signers <file>] [--integrator-name <name>]
                   show the project's plan graph as a tree, to see where
                   things stand. the roots are the outcomes with no
                   parent, the milestones, each with its progress: its
                   descendants that are terminal and not abandoned, out
                   of those not abandoned, as a count and a bar. under
                   each come its children, indented, in the order they
                   were created, then by title. each knot has a glyph for
                   its state (○ ready, ◐ in progress, ⊘ blocked, ◇
                   active, ✓ done, ✗ abandoned), its id and title, and,
                   while active, its claim and the claim's age (marked
                   stale past a day), its owner, and what blocks it. a
                   blocker that ended abandoned, discarded, superseded or
                   dropped clears the knot, and is marked with how it
                   ended. a reference to another project's knot,
                   <project>:<id>, is marked dangling when that
                   project's graph cannot be read or holds no such knot.
                   a root whose descendants are all terminal is one
                   line. three sections follow. waiting on you: the
                   active outcomes and bugs and the open questions the
                   operator owns, then the changes ready for
                   approval, as pending lists them. in flight: the
                   claimed knots, oldest claim first. open residuals:
                   the open bugs, under their parent. the operator is
                   git config valley.principal, or --owner. lines fit
                   the terminal as pending's do; glyphs are coloured on
                   a terminal unless NO_COLOR is set.
                     --project NAME  another project on the same host
                     --all        every project the serving host
                                  publishes, each under its name, as
                                  pending --all reads them. a project
                                  that cannot be read is reported in
                                  its own section
                     --owner P    read P as the operator
                     --json       print valley-plan/v1: every knot with
                                  what the graph derives of it, and the
                                  three sections (docs/architecture.md)
                     --known-signers FILE, --integrator-name NAME
                                  what a transfer statement is verified
                                  with, for the changes ready for
                                  approval, as changes reads them
                   needs the valley-go helper.
EOF
  help_runtime
}

help_checks() {
  cat <<'EOF'
usage: valley checks [--schema FILE] [--instance DIR] [--project DIR] [--names [--cost COST] | --json] [<base> [<head>]]

  checks [<base> [<head>]]
                   print the check names the composed verification policy
                   (dcr-f41f718) requires of the change between two
                   commits, which class made each one required, and the
                   change's carry mode: how far an approval of it carries
                   across a rebase, the strictest of exact, same-class and
                   same-paths among the classes it matched. It prints: no
                   check is run, nothing is gated, nothing is written.
                   base defaults to the merge base with main, head to HEAD.
                   by default both layers come from the target's tip
                   (origin/main), never from your checkout: the project's
                   own layer is policy/project there, and the owning valley
                   is named one line at policy/valley there, fetched and
                   read at policy/instance in that repository's integrated
                   tip. a valley's own config repository carries
                   policy/instance and uses it. a target carrying no
                   policy/project is derived under the floor alone; a floor
                   that is not there is refused. this is what the
                   integrator reads, so the answer is the same from any
                   branch, however stale. an option overrides each layer,
                   which is the only way to derive against a layer that
                   does not govern — the worked example under
                   examples/policy/, say:
                     --schema FILE    default $VALLEY_VERIFICATION_SCHEMA
                     --instance DIR   default the owning valley's floor
                     --project DIR    default policy/project at the target
                   naming a layer stops this reading the source it
                   replaced. the schema is the shape a policy is written
                   in rather than a layer, so it ships with this tool: the
                   package sets VALLEY_VERIFICATION_SCHEMA to its own copy.
                   with that unset, as when this script runs straight from
                   a the-valley checkout, it is schema/verification.cue in
                   the checkout.
                   a class's globs match bytes, whatever your locale
                   is: `?` is one byte, so `?.md` does not match
                   é.md, whose é is two.
                   after the report, two lines group the required checks
                   by the cost the policy declares, `quick` or `slow`. a
                   slow check takes minutes or needs special hardware. the
                   cost changes nothing required: approving runs every
                   required check whatever its cost, and the integrator
                   requires evidence for every one.
                     --names          print only the required check names,
                                      one per line
                     --cost COST      with --names, only the quick or
                                      the slow ones, e.g.
                                      `valley checks --names --cost slow`
                     --json           print the valley-checks/v1 document
                                      instead of the report
EOF
  help_runtime
}

